NNAAMMEE
     mmoouunntt__uuddff — mount a UDF filesystem

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     mmoouunntt__uuddff [--oo _o_p_t_i_o_n_s] _s_p_e_c_i_a_l _n_o_d_e

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The mmoouunntt__uuddff command attaches a UDF filesystem (typically found on a
     DVD) residing on the device _s_p_e_c_i_a_l to the global filesystem namespace at
     the location indicated by _n_o_d_e.  The filesystem is always mounted read‐
     only.  This command is invoked by mount(8) when using the syntax

         mmoouunntt [options] -t udf _s_p_e_c_i_a_l _n_o_d_e

     The options are as follows:

     --oo _o_p_t_i_o_n_s
             Options are specified with a --oo flag followed by a comma sepa‐
             rated string of options.  See the mount(8) man page for possible
             options and their meanings.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     mount(2), fstab(5), mount(8), umount(8), vnconfig(8)

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     UDF support first appeared in FreeBSD 5.0, and was then ported to
     OpenBSD 3.8.

